#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""castle-modal — the ambient-intake surface (docs/tasks/0009-ambient-intake.md).

The keybinding (Mod4+Shift+Return, see modules/home) opens a floating
`foot` terminal running this script. Stdlib Python only, same
constraint as agent/castle (agent/README.md) — and for the same reason:
this has to keep working with nothing installed beyond a python3
binary. It is deliberately NOT a GTK app, a wofi/fuzzel script, or
written in a second language; it's a terminal program because the
terminal is already the substrate this whole project is built on.

Five modes, all just callers of agent/castle's own record-writing and
record-reading functions — this file adds no judgment of its own,
exactly like `castle ask`/`castle digest` add none (Proposal 03):

  inbox    The one surface the one chord opens (docs/tasks/0034). Two
           views inside one window: compose (what the chord opens
           into, because the most common act is speaking) and an inbox
           list — `Tab` toggles between them. The list is everything
           waiting on the resident, derived with almost no new state:
           unanswered questions, undecided proposed changes, and
           results a read cursor (`$CASTLE_STATE_DIR/inbox-seen`, one
           id per line, append-only, appended only when a result's
           body is actually rendered) has not seen. A number opens an
           item — a question opens answer mode's flow, a change opens
           review mode's, a result renders its own body whole. Esc
           backs out one level; at the top it closes the window.
           `--focus ID` (scripts and the notification's deep link, the
           one caller that knows *why* the window opened) opens the
           inbox expanded on that record instead of opening compose.
  compose  Multi-line free text describing a problem, nothing else — no
           category picker, no priority dropdown. Ends on a lone '.'
           line or EOF. Becomes a `request` record via the same
           write_record() call `castle ask` uses; the modal is just
           another intake surface (docs/architecture.md: "the first
           intake is a CLI; a compositor keybinding opening a modal...
           are later intakes"). An interactive session is then asked
           one plain-language question — something to fix, or telling
           the system how it's doing — and the second answer routes
           the same body through castle's file_correction() instead
           (docs/tasks/0010-correction-record.md); intake itself still
           adds no judgment, since the resident is the one classifying,
           not the tool.
  status   A compact fold of recent errands: what was asked, what
           state it's in, what the system last decided and why. The
           "come back and check in later" surface. Corrections never
           appear here — they aren't errands, and there's nothing for
           the resident to check back on.
  answer   The questions the system is waiting on, and one of them
           answered (docs/tasks/0022-answer-in-ui.md; since
           docs/tasks/0034 this mode has no chord of its own — the
           inbox reaches the same flow, and this flag remains for
           scripts, CI, and a resident at a bare terminal).
           Oldest first, chosen by a single keypress, answered in the
           same text-entry grammar compose uses, filed through castle's
           file_answer() — the same write `castle answer` performs. The
           resident never types or reads a record id to do any of it,
           and nothing at all is written unless a question was chosen
           and a body typed.
  review   The first place a resident grants Castle anything
           (docs/tasks/0025-approval.md). Reached from the inbox (and
           from answer mode's picker) rather than a chord of its own: a
           question the harness filed to propose a configuration
           change branches out of answer mode's free-text grammar and
           into this, which shows where the change applies, says in
           the surface's own words what approving does and does not
           do, quotes Castle's account of why, prints the diff last
           and whole, and reads one keypress — approve, reject, or
           defer. **Nothing is activated by any of the three**, and
           nothing at all is done by rejecting or setting aside.
           Approving a change offered since
           docs/tasks/0026-apply-validate.md authorizes Castle to make
           it in the resident's own configuration repository and commit
           it there — no rebuild, no new generation, nothing pushed.
           A change offered before that task authorizes nothing but the
           record, permanently, and this surface says which of the two
           it is where the keys are rather than in a footnote.
           Activating is still 0027, and still nobody's but the
           resident's.

Headless by construction, not by special-casing: every mode reads
stdin and writes stdout/stderr with no dependency on a terminal at all.
Nothing here calls `curses` or emits raw ANSI codes except a couple of
purely cosmetic prompts guarded by `sys.stdin.isatty()` — delete those
lines and the program still does exactly the same work, which is the
property that lets CI pipe canned input at it with no compositor,
no foot, and no Sway involved (docs/tasks/0009's verification plan).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime as dt
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import os
import pathlib
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys


def _load_castle_module():
    """Import agent/castle (no .py extension, colocated in this same
    directory) as a library rather than shelling out to it — this
    script and `castle` are the same tool wearing two hats, so sharing
    the parser/writer in-process avoids re-implementing (and
    re-diverging from) the record format a second time.

    `spec_from_file_location` can't infer a loader from an extensionless
    filename, so the SourceFileLoader is constructed explicitly rather
    than relying on that inference.
    """
    castle_path = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "castle"
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("castle_lib", str(castle_path))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("castle_lib", castle_path, loader=loader)
    if spec is None or spec.loader is None:
        raise ImportError(f"cannot load {castle_path} as a module")
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


def _pause_for_dismissal(out=sys.stdout) -> None:
    """The keybinding (modules/home) opens a floating `foot` running this
    script as its sole command, and foot closes the instant that process
    exits — so without this, both the "Filed as <id>..." confirmation
    and the "empty request, nothing filed" refusal flash for zero
    human-perceptible frames (0009 review pass finding 5), on a surface
    whose entire premise is "describe it and walk away." Blocking on a
    keypress here — in the one process foot is running, rather than
    threading a shell wrapper's quoting through the Sway keybinding
    string — is what holds the window open until a human dismisses it.
    Gated on isatty() for the same reason compose mode's cosmetic
    prompts are: a piped-stdin run (CI, or `castle-modal` in a script)
    has no human to wait for and no tty to read a keypress from.

    **Both** ends, not just stdin (review round 1, finding 4). In foot —
    the only place this pause is for — stdin and stdout are the same
    tty. In a command substitution run from a terminal, `$(castle-modal
    ...)` pipes stdout while stdin stays the caller's tty, so a
    stdin-only test read as "a human is here" and blocked forever on a
    keypress nobody knew to press; a reviewer hung a harness on exactly
    that. Captured output means nobody is watching a window that could
    close, which is the whole thing this pause exists to prevent.
    """
    if not (sys.stdin.isatty() and sys.stdout.isatty()):
        return
    print(file=out)
    print("Press Enter to close.", file=out)
    try:
        input()
    except EOFError:
        pass


def _classify_interactively(out=sys.stdout) -> str:
    """One plain-language question, answered with one keypress
    (docs/tasks/0010-correction-record.md scope 6): is what was just
    typed something to fix, or is the resident telling the system how
    it's doing? Intake stays judgment-free — the resident classifies,
    the tool never guesses — and the words "correction record" never
    appear here or anywhere else this surface shows the resident;
    that vocabulary is for scripts and CI only (see --kind, below).

    termios cbreak, gated on isatty() by the same reasoning as every
    other cosmetic prompt in this file: a piped-stdin caller has no
    human to ask and no tty to read a keypress from, and never reaches
    this function. Bare Enter answers "something to fix" — the common
    case, and the one that shouldn't cost an extra keystroke.

    cbreak is engaged *before* the prompt is printed, not after
    (code-review finding, correction-record branch): printing is I/O —
    a syscall reaching the pty — and a keypress landing in the gap
    between "prompt printed" and "cbreak engaged" is held by the
    kernel's still-canonical line discipline until a newline arrives,
    which a bare single-character keypress never sends, hanging this
    read forever. Entering cbreak first collapses that window to a
    few bytecode instructions instead of a print() round-trip; output
    formatting is unaffected, since cbreak only changes how *input* is
    line-buffered, not how bytes written to the terminal are rendered.
    """
    import termios
    import tty

    fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    old_settings = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
    try:
        tty.setcbreak(fd)
        print(file=out)
        print("Is this something to fix, or are you telling me how I'm doing?", file=out)
        print("[Enter/f] something to fix        [t] telling you how you're doing", file=out)
        answer = sys.stdin.read(1)
    finally:
        termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old_settings)
    print(file=out)
    return "correction" if answer.lower() == "t" else "request"


def _read_typed_body(prefix: str = "") -> str:
    """This surface's one text-entry grammar: read stdin until a line
    containing exactly '.' (or EOF), then strip.

    Shared by compose and answer mode rather than written twice. One
    window, one way to type into it — a second grammar would be a second
    thing to learn about the same box, and the '.' terminator is already
    the thing a resident's fingers know.

    `prefix` is the inbox layout's one intrusion (docs/tasks/0034): its
    compose view peeks one keypress in cbreak to see whether the
    resident pressed Tab, and a peeked character that turns out to be
    ordinary text belongs at the front of the first line. The kernel's
    canonical line editing never buffered that character, so a
    backspace cannot erase it — consistent, as it happens, with what
    the terminal shows: the echo stays on screen and the character
    stays in the body.
    """
    lines: list[str] = []
    pending = prefix
    for raw_line in sys.stdin:
        line = pending + raw_line.rstrip("\n")
        pending = ""
        if line.strip() == ".":
            break
        lines.append(line)
    if pending:
        # EOF arrived immediately after the peeked character.
        lines.append(pending)
    return "\n".join(lines).strip()


# The compose prompt, in one place because two views print it (--mode
# compose and the inbox layout's compose view). "What do you need?"
# rather than "Describe the problem": the first real request through
# the system was a feature wish, not a problem, and the resident
# flagged the framing (docs/tasks/0034 §4). Same free-text,
# no-category-picker discipline as ever — only the presumption goes.
COMPOSE_PROMPT = "What do you need? Describe it in your own words."


def _print_compose_prompt(out) -> None:
    print(COMPOSE_PROMPT, file=out)
    print("End with a line containing just '.' (or Ctrl-D).", file=out)
    print(file=out)


def run_compose(castle, out=sys.stdout, kind: str = "request") -> int:
    if sys.stdin.isatty():
        _print_compose_prompt(out)
    return _file_composed(castle, _read_typed_body(), out, kind)


def _file_composed(castle, body: str, out, kind: str = "request") -> int:
    """The half of compose that happens after the body is captured:
    classify (interactively), write, confirm, pause. Split from
    run_compose so the inbox layout's compose view (docs/tasks/0034)
    files through the identical path rather than a second copy of it.
    """
    interactive = sys.stdin.isatty()
    if not body:
        print("castle-modal: empty request, nothing filed.", file=sys.stderr)
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 1

    # An interactive session is always asked, regardless of --kind (that
    # flag exists for non-interactive callers, who have no keyboard to
    # ask); a piped session keeps whatever --kind supplied (default
    # "request", so every existing piped caller is unchanged).
    if interactive:
        kind = _classify_interactively(out)

    # `write_record`'s own write-time refusals surface here as a
    # sentence, for the same reason answer mode reports them rather
    # than tracebacking: a compose window reached from inside a worker
    # turn is refused (Proposal 05 — only the resident may speak as the
    # resident), and a tenant that opened this window instead of the
    # CLI should meet the same explanation. The policy stays in the one
    # choke point; this is only the surface reporting it.
    try:
        if kind == "correction":
            record_id = castle.file_correction(body=body, refs=[], surface="modal")
            print(record_id, file=out)
            if interactive:
                print(f"Filed as {record_id}. Thanks — noted.", file=out)
        else:
            record_id = castle.write_record(
                castle.journal_dir(),
                type="request",
                provenance="requested",
                seat="intake",
                body=body,
            )
            print(record_id, file=out)
            if interactive:
                print(f"Filed as {record_id}. Check back with castle-modal --mode status.", file=out)
    except castle.RecordError as exc:
        print(str(exc), file=sys.stderr)
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 1
    _pause_for_dismissal(out)
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Answer mode (docs/tasks/0022-answer-in-ui.md). The third kind of
# resident speech this window carries: describing a problem (compose),
# volunteering feedback nobody asked for (compose's classification
# step), and — here — closing a question the system opened. Proposal 05
# says only the resident may close one; this mode's whole job is making
# that cheap without widening what counts as an answer.
#
# Everything this mode prints obeys one vocabulary rule: no record ids,
# and none of the words seat, provenance, refs, journal, record,
# channel, evidence, and no fact names. That binds the text this tool
# *adds*. The question and root-request bodies it shows are the worker's
# or the resident's own prose, shown verbatim and unfiltered.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Nine, because this is a picker meant to fit one screen, not a
# paginated list. Past nine, the overflow line names the count honestly
# rather than silently truncating.
ANSWER_PICKER_LIMIT = 9


def _first_line(text: str) -> str:
    stripped = text.strip()
    return stripped.splitlines()[0] if stripped else ""


def _pending_questions(records: dict) -> list:
    """Every `question` no answer names, oldest first.

    A flat pass over the journal, deliberately NOT the transitive errand
    walk `run_status` does: `_collect_downstream` follows `refs` from a
    root request, so a question whose refs chain never reaches one —
    which `castle record --type question` can legitimately produce, with
    no --refs at all — would never appear in an errand-keyed fold. A
    surface whose entire job is "nothing you are supposed to answer stays
    silently unanswerable" cannot inherit a fold that can hide a question
    by construction.

    Pending is the direct predicate, exact rather than heuristic: an
    answer's FIRST ref is the one question it closes, always, and the
    `answered` set below is the union of every answer's refs. It is the
    same test castle's own pendingness check and the status surface's
    "waiting on you" overlay apply.

    That set is deliberately a superset of the questions: since
    docs/tasks/0025-approval.md an answer deciding a proposed change
    refs `[question, result]`, so result ids are in it too, and
    `castle record --type answer --refs A,B` has always been able to
    put anything else in. It is safe here because it is only ever used
    to *exclude* — and only questions are tested against it, so a
    result id in the set matches nothing. **A reader widening this fold
    past `rec.type == "question"` must not inherit the assumption that
    every member of `answered` is a question that was answered.** The
    old wording of this paragraph said `file_answer` guaranteed exactly
    that, which was true when it was written and is not any more; it is
    corrected rather than deleted because a stated invariant is
    precisely what a later change would have relied on.

    Sorted by full id, which is chronological only to one-second
    granularity (review round 1, finding 7): `make_id` prefixes a
    UTC timestamp and appends a *random* suffix, so two questions filed
    in the same second sort by that suffix rather than by which came
    first. What the sort actually guarantees — and all the picker needs
    — is that the order is deterministic and identical on every
    invocation, so the number a resident presses means the same thing
    for as long as the list is on screen. Across seconds it is genuinely
    oldest-first, the same property `castle dispatch`'s own loop relies
    on.
    """
    answered = {ref for rec in records.values() if rec.type == "answer" for ref in rec.refs}
    return sorted(
        (rec for rec in records.values() if rec.type == "question" and rec.id not in answered),
        key=lambda rec: rec.id,
    )


def _is_proposal(castle, question) -> bool:
    """Is this question one the harness filed to propose a configuration
    change (docs/tasks/0025-approval.md §B)?

    The stamp, not the wording: a proposal question carries
    `proposal-sha256` and an ordinary elicited one never does. Nothing
    here greps the body — the same discipline `outcome` established for
    "did this turn fail," applied to "is this a change to decide."
    """
    return bool(question.fields.get(castle.PROPOSAL_SHA256_FIELD, "").strip())


def _authorizes_apply(castle, question) -> bool:
    """Does approving this proposal authorize applying it
    (docs/tasks/0026-apply-validate.md §A)?

    A second predicate beside `_is_proposal`, never a widening of it.
    `_is_proposal` decides pendingness, picker routing and whether the
    review branch is taken at all, and none of that may change: a
    proposal filed before docs/tasks/0026 is still a proposal and still
    gets decided. This one decides only which two sentences the surface
    says — the boundary statement and the confirmation — because those
    are what the resident's authority *means*, and it means something
    different for the two.

    Exact match on the one spelling, matching agent/castle's own
    `AUTHORIZES_APPLY_FIELD` rule and its fold: absence means no, an
    unrecognised value means no, and the safe direction is the default.
    """
    return question.fields.get(castle.AUTHORIZES_APPLY_FIELD, "").strip() == "true"


def _about_line(castle, records: dict, question) -> str:
    """The first line of the request this question came out of, or "" if
    the walk reaches no request. No placeholder and no error in that
    case — a question filed by hand with no chain to an errand is
    exactly the kind this mode must still be able to show."""
    root = castle._find_root_request(records, question)
    if root is None:
        return ""
    return _first_line(root.body)


def _show_picker(castle, records: dict, pending: list, out=sys.stdout, action: str = "answer"):
    """Print the picker and read the one keypress that answers it.

    `action` is the verb in the prompt, and exists only because review
    mode reuses this picker for proposed changes
    (docs/tasks/0025-approval.md §G): "press a number to answer" would
    name the wrong act there, on a surface whose whole subject is
    keeping the resident exact about what they are doing. The list,
    the ordering, the paging and the keypress grammar are identical —
    one picker, one thing to learn.
    Returns the chosen question record, or None for "close without
    answering."

    cbreak is engaged *before* anything is printed, for the reason
    `_classify_interactively` documents at length and this reuses
    verbatim: printing is I/O — a syscall reaching the pty — and a
    keypress landing in the gap between "prompt printed" and "cbreak
    engaged" is held by the kernel's still-canonical line discipline
    until a newline arrives, which a bare digit keypress never sends,
    hanging the read forever.

    The keypress is a screen-relative index, never a record id: 1..9 is
    what satisfies "no internal identifiers as required input", and it
    is safe to treat as stable *because* the ordering is deterministic
    and recomputed once per invocation, not because the numbers mean
    anything durable.

    Past nine, `m` shows the next nine and wraps around after the last
    page (review round 1, finding 1). The cap alone made question ten
    unreachable — possibly the exact question a notification had just
    pointed the resident at — on a surface whose whole claim is that it
    cannot hide a question by construction. Paging keeps the
    one-keypress grammar: digits pick, `m` turns the page, anything
    else closes.
    """
    import termios
    import tty

    fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    old_settings = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
    pages = max(1, (len(pending) + ANSWER_PICKER_LIMIT - 1) // ANSWER_PICKER_LIMIT)
    page = 0
    try:
        # cbreak stays engaged across every redraw, not just the first:
        # it is entered before anything is printed and left on until a
        # key ends the loop, so no page turn ever reopens the gap where
        # a keypress would be swallowed by the canonical line
        # discipline waiting for a newline that never comes.
        tty.setcbreak(fd)
        while True:
            start = page * ANSWER_PICKER_LIMIT
            shown = pending[start : start + ANSWER_PICKER_LIMIT]
            rest = len(pending) - len(shown)
            print("Waiting on you:", file=out)
            print(file=out)
            for index, question in enumerate(shown, start=1):
                print(f"  [{index}] {_first_line(question.body) or '(no text)'}", file=out)
                about = _about_line(castle, records, question)
                if about:
                    print(f"      about: {about}", file=out)
            if rest:
                print(file=out)
                print(f"…and {rest} more waiting — press m to see them.", file=out)
            print(file=out)
            if pages > 1:
                print(
                    f"Press a number to {action}, m for more, or any other key to close.",
                    file=out,
                )
            else:
                print(f"Press a number to {action}, or any other key to close.", file=out)
            key = sys.stdin.read(1)

            # Explicit membership, not `str.isdigit()` (review round 1,
            # finding 5): isdigit() is true of '²' and of Eastern-Arabic
            # digits, and `int()` accepts the latter — so a stray
            # superscript crashed the picker and '٣' silently selected
            # the third entry. The live range is this page's own, so a
            # digit past the end of a short last page closes rather than
            # indexing off it.
            if len(key) == 1 and key in "123456789"[: len(shown)]:
                # Always an explicit selection, even when one question is
                # pending: a uniform grammar keeps "answering something
                # you did not choose" impossible by construction rather
                # than by luck.
                selected = shown[int(key) - 1]
                break
            if pages > 1 and key == "m":
                # Wraps, so the last page's `m` returns to the first
                # rather than dead-ending on a key the prompt still
                # offers.
                page = (page + 1) % pages
                print(file=out)
                continue
            selected = None
            break
    finally:
        termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old_settings)
    print(file=out)
    return selected


def _answer_refusal(exc) -> str:
    """Plain-language wording for each AnswerRefused kind, in compose
    mode's existing "nothing filed" vocabulary and naming no ids — the
    same refusal `castle answer` renders with the record id in it, said
    the way this surface is allowed to say it."""
    if exc.kind == "already_answered":
        return "castle-modal: that question was already answered elsewhere, nothing filed."
    if exc.kind == "no_such_record":
        return "castle-modal: no such question, nothing filed."
    if exc.kind == "wrong_type":
        return "castle-modal: that is not a question, nothing filed."
    if exc.kind == "unreadable_answer":
        # No path and no id, unlike the CLI's version of this same
        # refusal: this surface may not hand the resident a filename to
        # go and fix. It says what happened and that nothing was
        # written, which is the honest part they can act on.
        return "castle-modal: can't check that question is still open, nothing filed."
    # The five from docs/tasks/0025-approval.md §C, in the same
    # vocabulary as the four above: no ids, no filenames, and none of
    # the internal words — a resident meeting one of these has to be
    # told what happened and that nothing was written, and nothing
    # else. "Change" is this surface's chosen noun throughout.
    if exc.kind == "not_a_proposal":
        return "castle-modal: that is not a proposed change, nothing filed."
    if exc.kind == "proposal_carries_fact":
        return (
            "castle-modal: deciding that change would also write down something "
            "about you, which deciding a change must never do. Nothing filed."
        )
    if exc.kind == "proposal_unresolvable":
        return "castle-modal: can't find the change that was proposed, nothing filed."
    if exc.kind == "proposal_stale":
        return (
            "castle-modal: that change is not what it was when it was proposed — "
            "it has been altered on disk since. Nothing filed."
        )
    if exc.kind == "proposal_needs_decision":
        return (
            "castle-modal: that is a proposed change, so it needs approve, reject "
            "or set aside rather than a written reply. Nothing filed."
        )
    return "castle-modal: empty answer, nothing filed."


def _run_answer_scripted(castle, question_id: str | None, out=sys.stdout) -> int:
    """The no-tty half of answer mode: --question or nothing.

    Guessing the oldest pending question in a script context would be a
    silent wrong-record path with no resident present to notice — worse
    than refusing outright. On success the answer's id goes to stdout:
    that is script output meant to be asserted on by a caller, not
    resident-facing prose, the same distinction `castle answer`'s own
    stdout already draws.
    """
    if not question_id:
        print(
            "castle-modal: questions are waiting, but this session has no terminal to "
            "choose one at — pass --question ID (scripts and CI only), or open the "
            "modal and pick.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    # A proposed change is refused here, early, with the right pointer
    # rather than the generic one (docs/tasks/0025-approval.md §G).
    # `file_answer` would refuse this write anyway — an ordinary answer
    # against a proposal question is `proposal_needs_decision` — but
    # the refusal a caller can act on is "you wanted the other mode",
    # and only this function knows that. The lookup tolerates an
    # unresolvable id: every other refusal on this path is
    # `file_answer`'s to give, and pre-empting them here would be two
    # implementations of the same checks.
    named = castle.load_journal_record(question_id)
    if named is not None and named.type == "question" and _is_proposal(castle, named):
        print(
            "castle-modal: that is a proposed change, and an ordinary answer would "
            "close it with no decision recorded — use --mode review --question ID "
            "(scripts and CI only), which approves, rejects or sets it aside.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    try:
        answer_id, _ = castle.file_answer(question_id=question_id, body=_read_typed_body())
    except castle.AnswerRefused as exc:
        print(_answer_refusal(exc), file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    except castle.RecordError as exc:
        # The write-time refusals `write_record` itself owns — today,
        # an answer attempted from inside a worker turn (Proposal 05,
        # docs/tasks/0023 §5). Not a policy check duplicated here: the
        # policy lives in the one choke point every writer passes
        # through, and this is only the surface that reports it, so a
        # tenant that reached for this window instead of the CLI meets
        # a sentence rather than a traceback.
        print(str(exc), file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print(answer_id, file=out)
    return 0


def run_answer(castle, out=sys.stdout, question_id: str | None = None) -> int:
    records = castle.load_all(castle.journal_dir())
    pending = _pending_questions(records)
    interactive = sys.stdin.isatty()

    # A scripted caller naming a question goes straight to the write,
    # whatever the fold says (review round 1, finding 2). The friendly
    # "nothing is waiting" exit used to come first for everyone, which
    # quietly turned every documented --question refusal — a bogus id, a
    # request id, an already-answered question — into exit 0 with prose
    # on stdout, because those are exactly the cases where the fold is
    # likely to be empty. file_answer does all the checking; this path
    # only has to let it.
    if question_id and not interactive:
        return _run_answer_scripted(castle, question_id, out)

    if not pending:
        print("Nothing is waiting on you.", file=out)
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 0

    if not interactive:
        return _run_answer_scripted(castle, None, out)

    # An interactive session always shows the picker and always ignores
    # --question. A flag that preselects a question in a session a human
    # is looking at is precisely the "answering the wrong one" hazard
    # this design exists to prevent; --question exists for a context
    # with no human present to choose, and this is not that context.
    question = _show_picker(castle, records, pending, out)
    if question is None:
        # The keypress itself IS the dismissal — exit 0, no pause,
        # nothing written anywhere. Dismissal is a successful use of
        # this surface (the resident looked and chose not to act), and
        # there is no code path from here to a write: file_answer is
        # called only below, after a question was chosen and a body
        # typed. Demanding a second "press Enter to confirm you meant to
        # leave" after a deliberate choice is the friction this surface
        # exists to avoid.
        return 0

    # A proposed configuration change is decided, not answered
    # (docs/tasks/0025-approval.md §G). The picker's own fold needed no
    # change to offer it — `_pending_questions` folds every unanswered
    # question and has never cared what one is about — and this is the
    # entire difference in what happens after it is picked. No new
    # chord: a resident who pressed Mod4+Shift+a to see what is waiting
    # on them should not have to know in advance which of two kinds of
    # waiting it was.
    if _is_proposal(castle, question):
        return run_review_for(castle, question, records, out, interactive=True)

    return _answer_flow(castle, question, records, out)


def _answer_flow(castle, question, records: dict, out=sys.stdout) -> int:
    """Show one chosen question whole and file the typed answer — the
    half of answer mode that runs after a question is in hand. Split
    from run_answer so the inbox (docs/tasks/0034) opens the identical
    flow rather than a second copy of it; every behavior below predates
    the split and is unchanged by it."""
    # The full body, verbatim, never truncated. The picker's first-line
    # preview is a convenience for choosing among several; letting a
    # resident answer on a truncated understanding of the question is a
    # different thing entirely, and docs/tasks/0010's "no seat
    # paraphrases" extends to a read as naturally as to a write.
    print(question.body.strip() or "(no text)", file=out)
    about = _about_line(castle, records, question)
    if about:
        print(f"about: {about}", file=out)
    print(file=out)
    print("Answer in your own words.", file=out)
    print("End with a line containing just '.' (or Ctrl-D).", file=out)
    print(file=out)

    try:
        _, recorded_fact = castle.file_answer(question_id=question.id, body=_read_typed_body())
    except castle.AnswerRefused as exc:
        # Empty body, or the raced case: the question was answered by
        # another window or another process between the picker showing
        # it and this keypress landing. No re-prompt loop — that is a
        # state machine this surface does not need and a hang risk on a
        # pty-driven program; the chord is one keypress away.
        print(_answer_refusal(exc), file=sys.stderr)
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 1
    except castle.RecordError as exc:
        # See the piped path above: `write_record`'s own refusals,
        # reported rather than re-implemented.
        print(str(exc), file=sys.stderr)
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 1

    print(file=out)
    # No record id (compose mode's "Filed as {id}." is the pattern being
    # deliberately broken here, not copied). The second sentence that
    # used to follow — "Nothing picks this errand back up automatically
    # yet." — is deleted by docs/tasks/0023-resume-cold.md, exactly as
    # the comment that stood here anticipated: an answered *blocking*
    # question now does resume its errand.
    #
    # Nothing replaces it. "This errand will continue" would be true
    # only for a blocking question, and only on a host where dispatch
    # is enabled and running — this process knows the first from the
    # question record but never the second, and it does not itself
    # trigger anything. Promising a continuation this surface cannot
    # observe would be docs/tasks/0015's defect the other way up: a
    # label asserting an action nobody here can see happen. A bare
    # "Filed." is true on every path.
    print("Filed.", file=out)
    if recorded_fact is not None:
        # A write into the resident's own model that the resident is
        # never told about is authority exercised invisibly. One plain
        # line is the minimum that makes it real to the person it is
        # about — and the internal "recorded resident-model entry for
        # fact '...'" line, which names the fact key, must never reach
        # this surface. When nothing was written, silence is the honest
        # report that nothing else happened.
        print("Noted — I'll remember that.", file=out)
    _pause_for_dismissal(out)
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Review mode (docs/tasks/0025-approval.md). The first place a resident
# grants Castle anything, and the reason every line below is written
# the way it is: what a resident is being asked for here is
# authorization, so the surface that asks has to be exact about what
# the authorization does — which today is nothing but be recorded.
#
# It obeys answer mode's vocabulary rule unchanged, with the same
# carve-out: no record ids, and none of the words seat, provenance,
# refs, journal, record, channel, evidence, question, answer, proposal,
# in anything this mode *adds*. The resident-facing noun for what is
# being decided is "a change", chosen once here so a later surface does
# not invent a third word. What the result record's own body says —
# Castle's account and the diff — is shown verbatim and unfiltered,
# exactly as question and request bodies already are.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Printed identically on every review, never model-generated, and
# printed WITH the keys rather than under the diff. Proposal 06's "the
# system may grade its own delivery, never its own judgment" given
# code: the same actor that produced the diff does not also get to
# assert what approving it does. And the direct fix for the defect this
# project keeps rediscovering — a label that causes the inaction it
# describes (docs/tasks/0015) — in its sharpest available form: an
# "Approve" that silently has no effect.
#
# **Two statements since docs/tasks/0026-apply-validate.md §A, selected
# by whether the question carries `authorizes-apply`, and the split is
# the whole mechanism rather than a presentation choice.** An applier
# that spent an approval granted under the older statement would be
# spending an authorization whose stated scope was "nothing is edited",
# and changing the wording afterwards does not reach backwards. So the
# old text is preserved verbatim below, used for a proposal filed
# before that task, because for those it remains exactly true; the new
# text is used for a proposal whose question says approving carries
# apply authority. Three surfaces changed in that task and this comment
# used to say two — the third is `_review_confirmation` below.
#
# Four decisions inside the new text, each deliberate:
#
#   * "authorizes Castle to make", not "makes". Whether the change is
#     applied promptly depends on `castle.agent.apply.enable`, which is
#     off by default. A screen that predicted an application that never
#     happens would be docs/tasks/0015's defect again; one that
#     predicted it correctly on some hosts and not others would be
#     worse. What approving *is* does not vary: it is the
#     authorization. What then happened is the applier's own record to
#     report.
#   * It does not promise a check. Validation is gated by a second
#     option that is also off by default, so "and then checks it
#     builds" would be the same overclaim in a smaller font.
#   * "without asking again", because there is no second confirmation
#     keypress. A resident should learn that from the screen rather
#     than from the consequence.
#   * "It pushes nothing anywhere", because "commit" is the word most
#     likely to be read as "publish", and pushing is an open authority
#     question nothing here touches (docs/architecture.md).
REVIEW_BOUNDARY_STATEMENT = """\
Approving this is you authorizing this exact change, and that
authorization is kept.

APPROVING IT AUTHORIZES CASTLE TO MAKE THIS CHANGE IN YOUR
CONFIGURATION REPOSITORY — to edit those files and commit them there,
on your say-so, without asking again. It pushes nothing anywhere.

NOTHING IS ACTIVATED AND NOTHING IS REBUILT: no new generation, and no
change to the machine you are using right now. Switching to the new
configuration stays yours to do, by hand.

Rejecting says this change is wrong. Setting it aside says you are not
deciding it now. Both close it for good — nothing expires, and nothing
decides on your behalf."""

# The statement every proposal filed before
# docs/tasks/0026-apply-validate.md was offered under, kept verbatim and
# used for exactly those. It is not legacy text to be retired on a
# schedule: it is what a resident was told, and for a question with no
# `authorizes-apply` stamp it stays true forever — the applier's fold
# will never touch one.
REVIEW_BOUNDARY_STATEMENT_PRE_APPLY = """\
Approving this is you authorizing this exact change, and that
authorization is kept.

NOTHING ON THIS MACHINE IS EDITED, COMMITTED, OR APPLIED BY APPROVING
IT — no file changes, no `git commit`, no rebuild, no new generation.
That is true for every change shown here today.

Rejecting says this change is wrong. Setting it aside says you are not
deciding it now. Both close it for good — nothing expires, and nothing
decides on your behalf."""

# The window this mode runs in opens at 720x480 (the fixed geometry
# modules/home's `window.commands` gives every castle-modal window),
# which cannot show a diff and an explanation at once. Rather than a
# second app_id with its own Nix rule — which would mean spawning a
# second process and handing off to it, since review mode runs inside a
# window that is already open and already mapped — the window resizes
# itself, best-effort, exactly the way `_fire_notification` fires:
# environment-overridable, silent on failure, and a no-op on a host
# with no Sway.
#
# **Scoped by app_id criteria, not "whatever is focused right now."** A
# bare `resize set` is focus-relative, and the window is only reliably
# focused at the instant of the keypress — a workspace switch or a
# resident who alt-tabbed while reading leaves it resizing and
# re-centring whatever they switched *to*, silently, from a tool whose
# whole subject is not acting without permission. Known, accepted edge
# case: two castle-modal windows open at once both match and both
# resize. Harmless — neither window's content changes, only its
# geometry — and not worth capturing this window's own container id
# from `swaymsg -t get_tree` to target `[con_id=N]` instead.
REVIEW_RESIZE_ENV = "CASTLE_REVIEW_RESIZE_COMMAND"
# A literal argv rather than a string to be split, so the criteria's
# quoting reaches swaymsg exactly as written instead of surviving a
# shlex round-trip. The environment override IS shlex-split, like
# CASTLE_NOTIFY_COMMAND, because an override is a whole command line a
# human or a harness typed.
REVIEW_RESIZE_ARGV = [
    "swaymsg",
    '[app_id="castle-modal"] resize set 1100 760, move position center',
]


def _resize_for_review() -> None:
    """Best-effort, never fatal, and never attempted without a terminal
    on both ends — the same double-tty gate `_pause_for_dismissal` uses,
    for the identical reason: a piped caller has no window to resize.

    `CASTLE_REVIEW_RESIZE_COMMAND` set to the empty string opts out
    explicitly, the same "don't even try" convention
    `CASTLE_NOTIFY_COMMAND` already has. A host with no Sway, no
    `swaymsg`, or a resident on another compositor gets a harmless
    failed exec and a review that still works at whatever size the
    window already is.
    """
    if not (sys.stdin.isatty() and sys.stdout.isatty()):
        return
    raw = os.environ.get(REVIEW_RESIZE_ENV)
    if raw is None:
        argv = list(REVIEW_RESIZE_ARGV)
    elif not raw.strip():
        return
    else:
        try:
            argv = shlex.split(raw)
        except ValueError:
            return
        if not argv:
            return
    try:
        subprocess.run(
            argv, check=False, timeout=5,
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass


def _split_proposal_body(castle, result) -> tuple[str, str, str]:
    """Take a result record apart into the three things this mode shows
    separately: where the change applies, Castle's account of why, and
    the diff.

    **The diff's boundary is the nonce in the record's own frontmatter,
    never a markdown fence in its body.** The first version of this
    function scanned for ```` ```diff ```` and ```` ``` ````, which is
    content being trusted to be structure — and a unified diff of a
    file that itself contains a fenced code block carries context lines
    that strip to exactly those, so the block closed early, the rest of
    the diff was relabelled as the machine's reasoning, and the
    resident was shown less than the change they were approving. That
    is the worst shape a defect can take on this surface, so the fix is
    structural rather than a stricter pattern: `run_worker_turn`
    generates eight random bytes *after* the tenant has finished
    producing the diff, wraps the diff in lines carrying them, and
    stamps them in the frontmatter. No diff content can contain a token
    that did not exist when the diff was written, and the frontmatter
    is somewhere a body cannot reach. `render_continuation_packet`
    solved the same problem the same way for the same reason
    (docs/tasks/0023); this is that idea applied to a record rather
    than to a stream, which is why the nonce has to be stored instead
    of merely generated.

    **Nothing here parses the tenant's prose.** The two other things
    this recognises are the harness's own writing — its opening line
    and the sentence naming the resolved checkout — and both are looked
    for only OUTSIDE the diff, which the boundary makes possible: a
    tenant that writes "This diff targets the ..." into a file this
    change touches can no longer have that line lifted out of the diff
    and shown as the harness's own. An earlier design (the decision
    pass's N20) wanted five parsed sections; that is exactly the parser
    this cannot have, since `agent/castle-worker-claude` tells a tenant
    only to "explain the reasoning on stdout" with no required
    headings, and a parser built against headings nobody promised would
    work today and break silently later.

    **The whole tail is dropped, not an enumeration of known lines.**
    `run_worker_turn` writes the tenant's own stdout *before* the
    boundary and appends only its own prose after it — the resolved-
    checkout sentence, the "no target was declared" notes, the
    undrained-output note, the mechanism-unavailable note, and
    `WORKER_PROPOSES_NOTE`. So everything after the closing boundary is
    harness-written by construction, and quoting any of it under
    "Castle's own account of why (its words, not verified by a person)"
    misattributes it by definition.

    An earlier version dropped exactly two of those lines by name and
    let the rest through, which is the enumeration this rule replaces:
    the mechanism-unavailable note reached the diagnosis carrying
    `castle.agent.repo.mechanism` and the configured filesystem path,
    an internal option name and a home-shaped path on a resident
    surface — the two things this surface may never print, the same two
    the opening line is dropped for. A list of known appended lines
    grows every time `run_worker_turn` learns to say something new, and
    the failure mode of forgetting to extend it is a leak on the one
    screen where authority is granted. A rule about where a line came
    from does not go stale.

    The resolved-checkout sentence is the single exception, lifted out
    as `where` rather than kept: it is shown under its own label, in
    the harness's own words, as the harness's — see `_render_proposal`.

    What this gives up is real and is named rather than hidden: on a
    turn whose output could not be collected (a setsid grandchild
    holding the pipes — rare, but possible on a `completed` turn), the
    harness's note saying so no longer reaches this screen, and since
    that same turn has no tenant reasoning either, the resident is
    shown a change with no account of why at all. That is the honest
    rendering of a turn that produced none. What must not happen is the
    resident reading the harness's explanation of the silence as the
    machine's reasoning about the change.

    **A result with no boundary stamped shows its body whole.** That is
    every result written before this field existed, and any a hand
    written proposal points at. Nothing is hidden and nothing is
    lifted: with no trustworthy way to say where the diff ends, this
    declines to guess rather than showing a resident three-quarters of
    a change under a heading that promises all of it.
    """
    body = result.body if result is not None else ""
    boundary = (
        result.fields.get(castle.DIFF_BOUNDARY_FIELD, "").strip()
        if result is not None
        else ""
    )
    if not boundary:
        return "", body.strip(), ""

    begin, end = castle.diff_boundary_lines(boundary)
    lines = body.splitlines()
    try:
        start = lines.index(begin)
        stop = lines.index(end, start + 1)
    except ValueError:
        # Stamped but not findable — a truncated or hand-edited record.
        # Same answer as no stamp at all: show everything, split
        # nothing.
        return "", body.strip(), ""

    diff_lines = lines[start + 1 : stop]
    # The markdown fence the harness writes just inside the boundary is
    # decoration, kept so the record still renders as a diff wherever a
    # journal is read as markdown. Dropped here only on an exact match,
    # so a body that somehow lacks it loses nothing but a reader's
    # tidiness.
    if diff_lines and diff_lines[0] == "```diff":
        diff_lines = diff_lines[1:]
    if diff_lines and diff_lines[-1] == "```":
        diff_lines = diff_lines[:-1]

    before = lines[:start]
    after = lines[stop + 1 :]

    where = ""
    kept: list[str] = []
    for index, line in enumerate(before):
        if index == 0 and line.startswith("Errand `"):
            continue
        kept.append(line)
    # The tail contributes exactly one thing and discards the rest: see
    # the docstring's "the whole tail is dropped" paragraph for why a
    # rule about provenance replaced a list of known lines here.
    #
    # Searched only in the tail, because that is where the harness
    # writes it — restricting the search is what keeps a tenant's own
    # prose, or a line of a file it is changing, from being promoted
    # into a sentence this surface presents as the mechanism's.
    for line in after:
        if not where and line.strip().startswith("This diff targets the "):
            where = line.strip()
            break
    return where, "\n".join(kept).strip(), "\n".join(diff_lines)


def _proposal_result(castle, question, records: dict):
    """The result record a proposal question is about, or None.

    `refs[1]` is the proposal, by the "first ref is lineage, later refs
    are context" convention (docs/tasks/0025-approval.md §A) — and the
    type is checked, not assumed, so this is the same predicate
    `file_answer` refuses on as `proposal_unresolvable`. One function,
    because the render and the pre-flight that decides whether to
    render at all must not be able to disagree about whether a proposal
    resolves.

    The fold is consulted first and the journal only as a fallback, the
    order the render has always used: a caller that already loaded the
    journal should not pay for a second read, and a question reached by
    id alone still resolves.
    """
    if len(question.refs) < 2:
        return None
    result = records.get(question.refs[1]) or castle.load_journal_record(
        question.refs[1]
    )
    if result is None or result.type != "result":
        return None
    return result


def _pending_proposals(castle, records: dict) -> list:
    """Every unanswered proposal, oldest first — `_pending_questions`
    narrowed to the ones this mode decides, with the same fold and the
    same ordering guarantee, so a change cannot be pending on one
    surface and absent from the other."""
    return [q for q in _pending_questions(records) if _is_proposal(castle, q)]


# Every C0/C1 control byte except newline and tab, including the ESC
# (0x1b) that starts every ANSI escape sequence. Newline and tab are
# the only control bytes this surface's own layout relies on; nothing
# it prints itself needs the rest, so the class is "everything but
# those two" rather than a denylist of specific sequences a future
# terminal could grow a new way to spell.
_UNSAFE_TERMINAL_CHARS_RE = re.compile("[\x00-\x08\x0b-\x1f\x7f-\x9f]")


def _defang_for_terminal(text: str) -> str:
    """Strip control bytes from tenant-authored text before it reaches the
    terminal a keypress is about to be read from.

    `_split_proposal_body` hands back the tenant's diagnosis and diff
    verbatim, by design — but "verbatim" cannot mean "byte for byte" on
    a surface that reads an approve/reject/defer keypress moments after
    printing them, in the same terminal. A changed file (or a model's
    own prose) containing a clear-screen or cursor-reposition escape
    sequence would repaint over the boundary statement and the key list
    an instant before the keypress is read, and the resident would be
    authorizing whatever was left on screen rather than what this
    function actually printed. Dropping every control byte but newline
    and tab leaves a sequence's parameter bytes as inert text — the
    ESC that would have made them live is gone — without touching how
    the diff or diagnosis reads.
    """
    return _UNSAFE_TERMINAL_CHARS_RE.sub("", text)


def _render_proposal(castle, question, records: dict, out) -> None:
    """What the resident reads before deciding, in the one order that
    survives having no structure to parse: evidence first, then what
    approving does and does not do beside the keys that do it, then
    Castle's own account, then the diff — last, verbatim, never
    truncated.

    No checklist anywhere, deliberately. Four sections, no checkboxes,
    and no "reversible? y/n" summary line asserting a property nothing
    here checked (Hodges et al., on checklists inverting the expertise
    ordering — docs/backlog/legible-history-calibrated-trust.md).
    """
    where, diagnosis, diff = _split_proposal_body(
        castle, _proposal_result(castle, question, records)
    )

    print(question.body.strip() or "(no text)", file=out)
    about = _about_line(castle, records, question)
    if about:
        print(f"about: {about}", file=out)
    print(file=out)

    if where:
        # Quoted, not re-derived. `run_worker_turn` already wrote this
        # exact fact in the harness's own words, beside the diff it is
        # about; restating it here in different words would be a second
        # implementation of one sentence, free to drift.
        print("Where this applies:", file=out)
        print(f"  {where}", file=out)
        print(file=out)

    # Selected by the question's own stamp, never by a date and never
    # by which text reads better: a proposal offered under the older,
    # narrower statement is decided under that statement, forever
    # (docs/tasks/0026-apply-validate.md §A).
    print(
        REVIEW_BOUNDARY_STATEMENT
        if _authorizes_apply(castle, question)
        else REVIEW_BOUNDARY_STATEMENT_PRE_APPLY,
        file=out,
    )
    print(file=out)
    # Three distinct, labelled, non-adjacent keys, printed here rather
    # than under the diff so that no resident can meet the keys without
    # meeting the sentence above them. Any other key — including bare
    # Enter — closes without deciding: there is no key whose accidental
    # press authorizes anything.
    print("  [a]pprove      [r]eject      [d]efer", file=out)
    print("  any other key closes this without deciding anything.", file=out)
    print(file=out)

    if diagnosis:
        # Whole and unparsed. Summarising it here would be exactly the
        # paraphrase docs/tasks/0010's "no seat paraphrases" forbids,
        # one surface over — applied to a read rather than a write.
        # Labelled, because a resident about to grant authority has to
        # know which of the words in front of them are a machine's.
        print("Castle's own account of why (its words, not verified by a person):", file=out)
        print(file=out)
        print(_defang_for_terminal(diagnosis), file=out)
        print(file=out)

    if diff:
        # The window resize above buys headroom, not a guarantee, and
        # `foot` has real scrollback that nothing otherwise tells a
        # resident exists. No pager: a $PAGER subprocess is new
        # machinery with its own failure modes — a missing binary, a
        # pager that eats the keypress this mode still has to read
        # afterwards — to solve a problem the terminal already solves.
        print(
            "Full diff below — scroll up in this window (Shift+Page Up in `foot`) "
            "if it runs past what's on screen.",
            file=out,
        )
        print(file=out)
        print(_defang_for_terminal(diff), file=out)
        print(file=out)


def _render_and_read(castle, question, records: dict, out, *, interactive: bool) -> str:
    """Print the whole review and read the one keypress that decides it.

    The two are one function because cbreak is engaged **before**
    anything is printed and left on until the key arrives — the same
    discipline `_show_picker` keeps across its own redraws, and for the
    same two reasons, both of which bite harder here. A keypress
    landing between the last print and the mode switch would be held
    by the still-canonical line discipline until a newline that a bare
    keypress never sends, hanging the read forever. And
    `tty.setcbreak` defaults to TCSAFLUSH, which *discards* whatever is
    already queued — so a resident who read the diff and pressed a key
    before this got around to switching modes would have that keypress
    thrown away rather than merely mistimed. This render is long, which
    makes both windows wide rather than microscopic.

    cbreak changes how input is line-buffered and nothing about how
    output is rendered, so holding it across the print costs nothing.

    A piped caller reads the same single character from stdin with no
    tty involved: `--question ID` is the scripted contract, and it
    decides with the same grammar rather than a second one.
    """
    if not interactive:
        _render_proposal(castle, question, records, out)
        return sys.stdin.read(1)
    import termios
    import tty

    fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    old_settings = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
    try:
        tty.setcbreak(fd)
        _render_proposal(castle, question, records, out)
        return sys.stdin.read(1)
    finally:
        termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old_settings)


# The keypress contract, in one place so the prompt above and the
# branch below cannot drift. Bare Enter is deliberately absent: a
# default decision reached by the key a resident presses to get out of
# things is the click-through habituation Proposal 06 warns about.
REVIEW_KEYS = {"a": "approve", "r": "reject", "d": "defer"}
REVIEW_CONFIRMATIONS = {
    "approve": "Approved.",
    "reject": "Declined.",
    "defer": "Set aside — it will not be offered again.",
}

# What `approve` confirms when the change carries apply authority. Only
# that one verdict has a second spelling: rejecting and setting aside
# mean exactly what they always meant, and giving them a second wording
# too would be churn dressed as symmetry.
REVIEW_CONFIRMATION_APPROVE_APPLYABLE = (
    "Approved. Castle will make this change in your configuration "
    "repository. Nothing will be activated."
)


def _review_confirmation(decision: str, *, authorizes_apply: bool) -> str:
    """The one line printed after a decision is written
    (docs/tasks/0026-apply-validate.md §A.2).

    A helper rather than a second dict, so the predicate that selects
    between the two approve wordings is spelled once, in the same place
    as the strings it selects between — the review surface's own third
    place that had to stop saying nothing is applied.
    """
    if decision == "approve" and authorizes_apply:
        return REVIEW_CONFIRMATION_APPROVE_APPLYABLE
    return REVIEW_CONFIRMATIONS[decision]


def run_review_for(castle, question, records: dict, out=sys.stdout, *, interactive: bool) -> int:
    """Decide exactly one proposed change, and never more than one per
    invocation (no batching, no approve-all: a keypress that spans more
    than one authorization is the "spray of decisions" this project's
    own posture warns against).

    Returns 0 for a decision and for a dismissal alike — looking and
    choosing not to decide is a successful use of this surface, and it
    writes nothing at all.

    **A proposal that resolves to nothing is refused before the keys
    are offered, not after they are pressed.** `file_answer` refuses it
    either way and nothing is ever written, so this is not a
    data-integrity fix; it is that a screen granting authority must not
    print the boundary statement, offer `[a]pprove [r]eject [d]efer`,
    and take a comment for a change it has already established it
    cannot show. With no result to split, `where`, the diagnosis and
    the diff were all empty — the resident was asked to authorize a
    blank. Reachable through the picker: a question carrying the stamp
    is pending whatever its `refs` say, which is the shape
    `modal-headless-test.sh`'s own `plant_bare_proposal` creates.

    The refusal is `file_answer`'s own `proposal_unresolvable`, raised
    here and rendered by `_answer_refusal`, so this pre-flight cannot
    come to say something different from the write path it is
    pre-empting.
    """
    if _proposal_result(castle, question, records) is None:
        print(
            _answer_refusal(
                castle.AnswerRefused("proposal_unresolvable", question_id=question.id)
            ),
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 1
    if interactive:
        _resize_for_review()
    key = _render_and_read(castle, question, records, out, interactive=interactive)
    decision = REVIEW_KEYS.get(key.lower()) if len(key) == 1 else None
    if decision is None:
        # Dismissal. Nothing written, no second "are you sure", no
        # durable trace — the journal cannot distinguish "shown four
        # times and walked away from" from "never opened", and that
        # cost is named rather than paid for with a receipt whose
        # observation window nothing here declared in advance
        # (Proposal 06).
        print(file=out)
        return 0

    body = ""
    if decision != "defer":
        # Genuinely optional, and only asked for where it might mean
        # something: `file_answer` no longer refuses an empty body when
        # a decision is present, so nobody has to type "ok" to approve.
        # Deferring asks for nothing at all — it is "not now", and
        # demanding a reason before letting the resident leave is
        # exactly the friction deferring exists to avoid paying.
        print(file=out)
        print("Anything you want to say about it? (optional)", file=out)
        print("End with a line containing just '.' (or Ctrl-D).", file=out)
        print(file=out)
        body = _read_typed_body()

    try:
        castle.file_answer(question_id=question.id, body=body, decision=decision)
    except castle.AnswerRefused as exc:
        # Including the two this surface exists to make survivable: the
        # change was altered on disk since it was shown, or another
        # window decided it while this one was open. No re-prompt loop
        # — the chord is one keypress away.
        print(_answer_refusal(exc), file=sys.stderr)
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 1
    except castle.RecordError as exc:
        print(str(exc), file=sys.stderr)
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 1

    print(file=out)
    print(
        _review_confirmation(
            decision, authorizes_apply=_authorizes_apply(castle, question)
        ),
        file=out,
    )

    # Re-read rather than reasoned about: another window may have
    # decided something while this one was open, and the count a
    # resident is given has to be the count that is true now.
    remaining = [
        proposal
        for proposal in _pending_proposals(castle, castle.load_all(castle.journal_dir()))
        if proposal.id != question.id
    ]
    if remaining:
        # Said, and then this exits — it does not loop into the next
        # one. Looping would be the spray of decisions again, and
        # reopening the list is one chord away. CHORD COUPLING:
        # Mod4+Shift+Return is prose here and a real binding in
        # modules/home/default.nix's keybindings attrset; change one
        # and grep for the other (docs/tasks/0034 removed the answer
        # chord this line used to name).
        noun = "change" if len(remaining) == 1 else "changes"
        print(
            f"{len(remaining)} more proposed {noun} waiting — press "
            "Mod4+Shift+Return to see them.",
            file=out,
        )
    _pause_for_dismissal(out)
    return 0


def run_review(castle, out=sys.stdout, question_id: str | None = None) -> int:
    """`--mode review`, entered directly rather than through the answer
    picker. The picker route (run_answer) is how a resident actually
    gets here; this exists so the same decision is reachable from a
    script, from CI, and from a resident with no working display —
    exactly the parity every other write path in this tool already has.
    """
    records = castle.load_all(castle.journal_dir())
    pending = _pending_proposals(castle, records)
    interactive = sys.stdin.isatty()

    # A scripted caller naming one goes straight to it, whatever the
    # fold says — the same ordering answer mode settled on (review
    # round 1, finding 2) and for the same reason: an empty fold is
    # exactly when a script is most likely to hit a documented
    # refusal, and letting the friendly "nothing waiting" exit come
    # first would turn every one of them into exit 0 with prose.
    if question_id and not interactive:
        question = castle.load_journal_record(question_id)
        if question is None or question.type != "question":
            print(_answer_refusal_for_missing(question), file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        if not _is_proposal(castle, question):
            print(
                "castle-modal: that is not a proposed change — an ordinary question "
                "is closed with --mode answer --question ID. Nothing filed.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 1
        return run_review_for(castle, question, records, out, interactive=False)

    if not pending:
        print("No changes are waiting for your decision.", file=out)
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 0

    if not interactive:
        # Guessing in a script context is the silent wrong-record path
        # `--question` exists to prevent, and the stakes are higher
        # here than in answer mode: the wrong guess would be an
        # authorization.
        print(
            "castle-modal: this session has no terminal to choose a change at — "
            "pass --question ID (scripts and CI only), or open the modal and "
            "review it there.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1

    # An interactive session ignores --question and chooses for itself,
    # the same rule answer mode already applies and for the same
    # reason: preselecting for a human who is looking at the screen is
    # the deciding-the-wrong-one hazard the picker exists to remove.
    # With exactly one waiting there is nothing to choose between, so
    # the oldest — the only — one is shown directly.
    if len(pending) == 1:
        question = pending[0]
    else:
        question = _show_picker(castle, records, pending, out, action="review")
        if question is None:
            return 0
    return run_review_for(castle, question, records, out, interactive=True)


def _answer_refusal_for_missing(question) -> str:
    """The two shapes `run_review`'s scripted path can meet before
    `file_answer` ever gets a look, said in this surface's vocabulary
    and matching `_answer_refusal`'s wording for the same two kinds."""
    if question is None:
        return "castle-modal: no such question, nothing filed."
    return "castle-modal: that is not a question, nothing filed."


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inbox layout (docs/tasks/0034-inbox-modal.md). One chord, one
# surface: compose view (what the chord opens into) and an inbox list,
# `Tab` between them. The list is derived, not stored — with one
# deliberate exception, the read cursor below, because "unread result"
# is a notion the append-only journal cannot hold.
#
# The same vocabulary rule as answer mode binds everything this layout
# adds: no record ids, and none of the internal words. Bodies are shown
# verbatim, as everywhere else.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def _inbox_seen_path(castle):
    """$CASTLE_STATE_DIR/inbox-seen — beside journal/, never inside it.
    It is state, not a record: nothing about a result changes when the
    resident reads it, so seen-ness lives with the surface that owns
    it, the same shape as the dispatch watermark's "began here" fact.
    It syncs with the state repo (seen-ness follows the resident, not
    the machine), and losing it costs a few once-read results
    reappearing as unread — the cheap direction to fail in."""
    return castle.state_dir() / "inbox-seen"


def _read_seen(castle) -> set:
    try:
        text = _inbox_seen_path(castle).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        return set()
    return {line.strip() for line in text.splitlines() if line.strip()}


def _mark_seen(castle, record_id: str, seen: set) -> None:
    """Append-only, like the journal it sits beside: one id per line,
    never rewritten, appended only when a result's body was actually
    rendered — never merely because a list containing it was shown.
    Skipping an id already present is not a rewrite; it just keeps a
    re-read from growing the file."""
    if record_id in seen:
        return
    path = _inbox_seen_path(castle)
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
        handle.write(record_id + "\n")
    seen.add(record_id)


# The resident-facing label for each kind of waiting thing. "an answer
# to read" for a result, matching the notification's own noun ("your
# request has an answer") — the journal's word `result` is not this
# surface's to use.
_INBOX_LABELS = {
    "question": "needs your answer",
    "proposal": "a change to review",
    "result": "an answer to read",
}


def _inbox_items(castle, records: dict, seen: set) -> list:
    """Everything waiting on the resident, in the brief's order:
    questions first (the turn may be suspended on you — blocking ones
    ahead of the rest, oldest first within each), then undecided
    proposed changes, then results the read cursor has not seen.
    Nothing else: closed items belong to `castle digest`, not here.

    Questions and proposals reuse `_pending_questions`' fold — pending
    iff no answer names it — so this list, the answer picker, and the
    status overlay can never disagree about one question. The cursor is
    consulted for results only: pending-ness of the other two kinds is
    derivable, and deriving beats recording wherever possible.
    """
    pending = _pending_questions(records)
    questions = sorted(
        (q for q in pending if not _is_proposal(castle, q)),
        key=lambda q: (
            q.fields.get(castle.BLOCKING_FIELD, "").strip() != "true",
            q.id,
        ),
    )
    proposals = [q for q in pending if _is_proposal(castle, q)]
    unread = sorted(
        (rec for rec in records.values() if rec.type == "result" and rec.id not in seen),
        key=lambda rec: rec.id,
    )
    return (
        [("question", rec) for rec in questions]
        + [("proposal", rec) for rec in proposals]
        + [("result", rec) for rec in unread]
    )


def _print_inbox_entries(castle, records: dict, shown: list, out) -> None:
    for index, (kind, rec) in enumerate(shown, start=1):
        label = _INBOX_LABELS[kind]
        if kind == "result":
            # A result's own first line is the harness's turn header —
            # a record id and a store path, the two things this surface
            # may never print. The originating request's words identify
            # it instead, exactly as the notification does.
            print(f"  [{index}] {label}", file=out)
        else:
            print(f"  [{index}] {label}: {_first_line(rec.body) or '(no text)'}", file=out)
        about = _about_line(castle, records, rec)
        if about:
            print(f"      about: {about}", file=out)


def _render_result_body(castle, rec, seen: set, out) -> None:
    """The record body, whole and verbatim (defanged of control bytes,
    for the reason review mode documents on `_defang_for_terminal` — a
    keypress is read from this same terminal moments later). Since
    docs/tasks/0023 and 0033 the body already carries everything a cold
    reader needs. Rendering it is what spends the read cursor: the id
    is appended the moment the body is on screen, not when a list
    mentioning it was."""
    print(_defang_for_terminal(rec.body.strip() or "(no text)"), file=out)
    _mark_seen(castle, rec.id, seen)


def _read_one_key_cbreak(out, *lines) -> str:
    """Print `lines` and read one keypress, cbreak engaged before the
    first print — the same discipline `_classify_interactively`
    documents at length and every keypress reader in this file obeys."""
    import termios
    import tty

    fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    old_settings = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
    try:
        tty.setcbreak(fd)
        for line in lines:
            print(line, file=out)
        return sys.stdin.read(1)
    finally:
        termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old_settings)


def _result_view(castle, rec, seen: set, out) -> None:
    """One result, read in place. Esc backs out to the list — one level,
    per the brief — and every other key is ignored rather than acting:
    a reflex keypress on a reading surface must not do anything, the
    same posture the review keys take."""
    import termios
    import tty

    print(file=out)
    fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    old_settings = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
    try:
        tty.setcbreak(fd)
        _render_result_body(castle, rec, seen, out)
        print(file=out)
        print("Esc to go back.", file=out)
        while sys.stdin.read(1) != "\x1b":
            pass
    finally:
        termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old_settings)
    print(file=out)


def _show_inbox_list(castle, records: dict, items: list, out):
    """Print the inbox and read the keypress that acts on it. Returns
    ("open", (kind, rec)), ("compose", None) or ("close", None).

    The grammar is the picker's, extended by exactly one key: digits
    pick, `m` pages past nine, `Tab` switches to compose, and any other
    key closes the window writing nothing — the keypress is the
    dismissal, as everywhere else in this file. There is deliberately
    no key that opens an item without naming it: opening a result
    spends the read cursor, and a reflex Enter must not mark something
    read that nobody read.
    """
    import termios
    import tty

    fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    old_settings = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
    pages = max(1, (len(items) + ANSWER_PICKER_LIMIT - 1) // ANSWER_PICKER_LIMIT)
    page = 0
    try:
        tty.setcbreak(fd)
        if not items:
            # An empty inbox says so and offers compose
            # (docs/tasks/0034 §1): the friendly line answer mode
            # already uses, plus the way back to the common act.
            print("Nothing is waiting on you.", file=out)
            print(file=out)
            print("Press Tab to write something, or any other key to close.", file=out)
            key = sys.stdin.read(1)
            return ("compose", None) if key == "\t" else ("close", None)
        while True:
            start = page * ANSWER_PICKER_LIMIT
            shown = items[start : start + ANSWER_PICKER_LIMIT]
            rest = len(items) - len(shown)
            print("Waiting on you:", file=out)
            print(file=out)
            _print_inbox_entries(castle, records, shown, out)
            if rest:
                print(file=out)
                print(f"…and {rest} more waiting — press m to see them.", file=out)
            print(file=out)
            if pages > 1:
                print(
                    "Press a number to open, Tab to write something, m for more, "
                    "or any other key to close.",
                    file=out,
                )
            else:
                print(
                    "Press a number to open, Tab to write something, or any other "
                    "key to close.",
                    file=out,
                )
            key = sys.stdin.read(1)
            if len(key) == 1 and key in "123456789"[: len(shown)]:
                return ("open", shown[int(key) - 1])
            if key == "\t":
                return ("compose", None)
            if pages > 1 and key == "m":
                page = (page + 1) % pages
                print(file=out)
                continue
            return ("close", None)
    finally:
        termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old_settings)


def _open_inbox_item(castle, kind: str, rec, records: dict, seen: set, out):
    """Open the view an item needs (docs/tasks/0034 §1): a question is
    answer mode's flow, a change is review mode's, a result is its own
    body. Returns an exit code to end the invocation with, or None to
    return to the list (only the result view backs out — the two
    writing flows keep their existing close-when-done semantics, and
    the chord is one keypress away)."""
    if kind == "question":
        return _answer_flow(castle, rec, records, out)
    if kind == "proposal":
        return run_review_for(castle, rec, records, out, interactive=True)
    _result_view(castle, rec, seen, out)
    return None


def _open_focus_target(castle, focus: str, records: dict, seen: set, out):
    """What `--focus ID` opens — the one case where the surface knows
    why it was opened, so it opens there. Returns an exit code, or None
    to fall through to the inbox list (a focused result backs out to it;
    anything unopenable degrades to the list with one plain line, never
    an error — the notification that carried this id may be hours old,
    and the world may have moved on)."""
    rec = records.get(focus) or castle.load_journal_record(focus)
    if rec is None:
        print("That one isn't here any more.", file=out)
        print(file=out)
        return None
    if rec.type == "result":
        _result_view(castle, rec, seen, out)
        return None
    if rec.type == "question":
        answered = {
            ref for other in records.values() if other.type == "answer" for ref in other.refs
        }
        if rec.id in answered:
            print("That one is already handled.", file=out)
            print(file=out)
            return None
        if _is_proposal(castle, rec):
            return run_review_for(castle, rec, records, out, interactive=True)
        return _answer_flow(castle, rec, records, out)
    print("That one isn't something to open.", file=out)
    print(file=out)
    return None


def _compose_view(castle, waiting_count: int, out):
    """The inbox layout's compose view: today's compose mode plus one
    static line above the prompt when items are waiting — a count and a
    key, nothing else. Static information, not initiative; where the
    boundary to volunteering help lies is recorded in
    docs/backlog/the-modal-could-choose-its-opening-view.md.

    One keypress is peeked in cbreak before the line grammar starts:
    Tab switches to the inbox, Esc closes (compose's discard semantics
    — nothing typed, nothing written, exit 0, the keypress is the
    dismissal), and anything else is the first character of the body,
    handed to `_read_typed_body` as its prefix. Once typing has begun,
    Tab is just a character — text may legitimately contain one.

    Returns ("inbox", None) or ("exit", code).
    """
    lines = []
    if waiting_count:
        lines.append(f"{waiting_count} ready — Tab to view")
        lines.append("")
    lines.append(COMPOSE_PROMPT)
    lines.append("End with a line containing just '.' (or Ctrl-D).")
    lines.append("")
    first = _read_one_key_cbreak(out, *lines)
    if first == "\t":
        print(file=out)
        return ("inbox", None)
    if first == "\x1b":
        return ("exit", 0)
    if first in ("\x04", ""):
        # Ctrl-D (or EOF) straight away: the existing empty-body
        # refusal path, unchanged.
        return ("exit", _file_composed(castle, "", out))
    prefix = "" if first in ("\n", "\r") else first
    return ("exit", _file_composed(castle, _read_typed_body(prefix), out))


def _run_inbox_piped(castle, records: dict, seen: set, focus: str | None, out) -> int:
    """The no-tty half of inbox mode: the list, printed once, or the
    focused record's body. Read-only with one exception — rendering a
    focused result's body spends the read cursor, piped or not, because
    "appended when the body is rendered" is a rule about the mechanism,
    not about who is watching (the same headless-by-construction
    posture the whole file takes). Scripts that want to *act* on an
    item already have --mode answer and --mode review."""
    if focus:
        rec = records.get(focus) or castle.load_journal_record(focus)
        if rec is None:
            print("castle-modal: nothing to open there.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        if rec.type == "result":
            _render_result_body(castle, rec, seen, out)
            return 0
        print(rec.body.strip() or "(no text)", file=out)
        return 0
    items = _inbox_items(castle, records, seen)
    if not items:
        print("Nothing is waiting on you.", file=out)
        return 0
    print("Waiting on you:", file=out)
    print(file=out)
    _print_inbox_entries(castle, records, items, out)
    return 0


def run_inbox(castle, out=sys.stdout, focus: str | None = None) -> int:
    seen = _read_seen(castle)
    records = castle.load_all(castle.journal_dir())
    if not sys.stdin.isatty():
        return _run_inbox_piped(castle, records, seen, focus, out)

    # The chord opens compose — the most common act is speaking, and
    # the chord carries no information about why it was pressed. A
    # --focus caller is the one opener that does know why, so it opens
    # the inbox instead, expanded on the record it was told about.
    view = "compose"
    if focus:
        code = _open_focus_target(castle, focus, records, seen, out)
        if code is not None:
            return code
        view = "inbox"
        records = castle.load_all(castle.journal_dir())

    while True:
        items = _inbox_items(castle, records, seen)
        if view == "compose":
            outcome, code = _compose_view(castle, len(items), out)
            if outcome == "exit":
                return code
            view = "inbox"
            continue
        action, picked = _show_inbox_list(castle, records, items, out)
        if action == "close":
            return 0
        if action == "compose":
            print(file=out)
            view = "compose"
            continue
        code = _open_inbox_item(castle, picked[0], picked[1], records, seen, out)
        if code is not None:
            return code
        # Back from a result view: re-derive before redrawing, so a
        # question answered from another window meanwhile is not still
        # offered here.
        records = castle.load_all(castle.journal_dir())


def _stamp_of(record_id: str) -> str:
    """The UTC timestamp a record id begins with, and nothing else.

    Ids are `<stamp>-<type>-<random suffix>`, so comparing two of them
    whole compares the type name whenever the stamps tie — which makes
    `...-answer-...` older than a `...-result-...` written in the same
    second, by alphabet rather than by time. Callers that mean "which
    happened later" want this; callers that only need a deterministic
    total order (the picker) are right to use the whole id.
    """
    return record_id.split("-", 1)[0]


def _started_at(castle, claim) -> str:
    """A claim's start time as local wall-clock `HH:MM`.

    `created` is UTC, always (agent/castle's CREATED_FMT), and "started
    14:03" means nothing to a resident if 14:03 is in a timezone they
    don't live in. Falls back to the raw field if it doesn't parse —
    this is a status line, not a validator."""
    raw = claim.fields.get("created", "")
    try:
        when = dt.datetime.strptime(raw, castle.CREATED_FMT).replace(tzinfo=dt.timezone.utc)
    except ValueError:
        return raw or "?"
    return when.astimezone().strftime("%H:%M")


def _outcome_label(outcome: str, request_id: str) -> str:
    """One result's `outcome` as a resident-facing label.

    Every non-completed value names the retry command explicitly. That
    is docs/tasks/0015's lesson restated — "the label must not cause
    the inaction it describes": a label honest about failure but silent
    about what to do next repeats the same mistake in the other
    direction. An absent `outcome` means "done", deliberately and
    forever: every result written before that field existed is already
    permanent in an append-only journal and cannot be backfilled, so a
    pre-existing result whose body says "FAILED" in prose keeps reading
    as "done" — the same backward-compatibility posture
    `considered`/`propensity` already established. Nothing here greps a
    body for a word like "FAILED"; the field is the fact.
    """
    if outcome == "failed":
        return f"failed — castle work {request_id} to retry"
    if outcome == "timeout":
        return f"timed out — castle work {request_id} to retry"
    if outcome == "interrupted":
        return f"interrupted — castle work {request_id} to retry"
    if outcome and outcome != "completed":
        # An outcome from outside today's vocabulary — a later task
        # extending the contract, a hand-written record, a typo. Shown
        # verbatim with the retry hint rather than folded into "done":
        # `outcome` is a named cross-task contract (agent/README.md),
        # and defaulting an unrecognised member of it to success is the
        # exact failure this whole field exists to end — a surface
        # quietly reporting an errand as fine because it did not
        # understand what it was told. Absent and `completed` keep
        # meaning done, which is the append-only compatibility promise
        # and unchanged.
        return f"{outcome} — castle work {request_id} to retry"
    return "done"


# Why the applier declined, in the resident's own vocabulary, for the
# one label shape that needs a clause rather than a phrase. Kept beside
# the labels below rather than derived from the record's body: a body is
# prose written for reading, and a status line is a fold.
_APPLY_REFUSAL_REASONS = {
    "refused-target-mechanism": (
        "it changes the Castle Turing framework, not your own configuration"
    ),
    "refused-artifact-changed": "what is on disk is no longer what you approved",
    "refused-no-patch": "no exact copy of the change was kept",
    "refused-patch-stale": "it no longer fits your configuration repository",
    "refused-tree-dirty": "you have uncommitted edits to the same files",
}


def _apply_outcome_label(apply_outcome: str) -> str:
    """What happened to an approved change, as a resident-facing label
    (docs/tasks/0026-apply-validate.md §H).

    "not activated" appears in four of these on purpose. It is the one
    fact the whole task turns on, and a status line is exactly where a
    resident would otherwise assume otherwise.

    An unrecognised value renders verbatim rather than collapsing into
    any of the nine, following `_outcome_label`'s own rule above and for
    the reason it gives: a surface quietly reporting a state because it
    did not understand what it was told is the failure both closed
    vocabularies exist to prevent.
    """
    fixed = {
        "applied-validated": "applied and checked — not activated",
        "applied-unvalidated": "applied, not checked — not activated",
        "validation-failed": "applied, and the check failed — not activated",
        "applied-uncommitted": "partly applied — see your configuration repository",
    }.get(apply_outcome)
    if fixed:
        return fixed
    reason = _APPLY_REFUSAL_REASONS.get(apply_outcome)
    if reason:
        return f"approved, but not applied — {reason}"
    return apply_outcome


def _errand_state(castle, request, downstream: list, records: dict) -> str:
    """`result` was checked before question/answer, so 'done' won any
    errand that also had an unanswered question — but the worker prompt
    (agent/castle-worker-claude) explicitly tells tenants to "file the
    question alongside your result rather than stopping", so
    result+unanswered-question is the *normal* shape for a worker that
    needed input mid-errand, not an edge case. That made "waiting on
    you" — the one state this whole modal exists to surface — silently
    unreachable in exactly the flow the worker is instructed to use
    (0009 review pass finding 4). Reporting both when both apply is
    strictly more honest than picking one. The ", waiting on you"
    overlay below composes exactly as it always has — and it is now
    computed by pairing each question with an answer that names it,
    rather than by asking whether the fold contains an answer of any
    kind. The old form went quiet after the first answer, so a worker
    that raised a second question on the same errand waited on a
    resident who was never told.

    **The errand's state is the state of its newest turn.** Since
    docs/tasks/0021-auto-dispatch.md a result names the `claim` it
    closes, so "which turn are we talking about" is answerable — and it
    has to be asked, because keying on the newest *result* gets two
    real cases backwards. An errand whose retry completed, and whose
    older abandoned turn was reaped afterwards, has a newer
    `interrupted` result than its `completed` one: keyed on results it
    reads as interrupted forever, though it is finished. And an errand
    with a second turn that died leaves a claim no result closes, which
    an older result masks entirely. Older turns' accounts — reaped ones
    included — are history; the newest turn is the current truth. A
    journal with no `claim` records at all (everything written before
    this task) keeps the old newest-result behavior unchanged.

    "in progress" is now *earnable* (docs/tasks/0021 §4), which
    docs/tasks/0015 deliberately left it not being: a `claim` record
    says a worker turn began, and a live `flock` on that errand's lease
    says one is running right this second. Both, not either — a claim
    with a dead lease is an interrupted turn, and saying "in progress"
    about it would be exactly the absence-of-evidence guess 0015
    removed. It is also checked **first**, ahead of any result: an
    errand whose failed turn the resident is retrying right now must
    not be labelled with advice to run the very command that is already
    running (and would be refused the lease).

    The narrow race this live probe shares with the dispatch reaper's
    own probe is documented on agent/castle's `lock_is_held`, and
    accepted there.
    """
    request_id = request.id

    # Every question in this fold that no answer names. The old form
    # was `"question" in types and "answer" not in types`, which never
    # paired an answer with its question: one answered question made
    # the errand stop saying "waiting on you" forever, hiding every
    # later question the worker raised. Questions and answers are the
    # one thing that legitimately arrives here through the transitive
    # walk (a follow-up request's question is still this errand chain
    # waiting on the resident), so unlike the turn state below they are
    # deliberately NOT keyed to this request id.
    #
    # **`answered` comes from a flat pass over the whole journal, not
    # from `downstream`, so that three surfaces cannot disagree about
    # one question.** `file_answer`'s duplicate guard scans every
    # answer file in the journal, and `_pending_questions` folds every
    # answer record — both treat a question as closed if *any* answer
    # names it. Taking this one from the errand walk made it the odd
    # one out the moment docs/tasks/0023 narrowed that walk to the
    # lineage edge: an answer written through the generic door as
    # `--refs Q_A,Q_B` sits in A's fold and not in B's, so B's overlay
    # would say "waiting on you — press Mod4+Shift+a to answer" forever
    # while the picker, folding flat, correctly refused to offer Q_B
    # because it is answered. Telling a resident to answer something
    # the answer surface will not show them is docs/tasks/0015 scope 3
    # exactly, and it would have been this branch's own doing. Which
    # questions belong to this errand still comes from `downstream` —
    # that is an errand question — but whether one is answered is a
    # property of the question, and every surface now reads it alike.
    answered = {
        ref for rec in records.values() if rec.type == "answer" for ref in rec.refs
    }
    waiting_questions = [
        rec for rec in downstream if rec.type == "question" and rec.id not in answered
    ]
    waiting = bool(waiting_questions)

    # `_collect_downstream` is transitive over `refs` with no keying by
    # type or by errand, which is right for the fold as a whole and
    # wrong for deriving *this* errand's turn state from it. A
    # follow-up filed with `castle ask --refs R1` pulls R2 into R1's
    # downstream, and R2's claims and results come with it — so an R1
    # that finished cleanly could be labelled from R2's abandoned turn
    # ("interrupted — castle work R1 to retry", about a turn that was
    # never R1's). Both selections are keyed to this request, the same
    # way agent/castle keys them: a claim belongs to the errand named
    # first in its `refs` (what the reaper uses to find the request),
    # and a result belongs to any errand it names (what `closing_result`
    # uses, and what lets `--refs R,C` close a turn by hand).
    results = sorted(
        (rec for rec in downstream if rec.type == "result" and request_id in rec.refs),
        key=lambda rec: rec.id,
    )
    claims = sorted(
        (
            rec
            for rec in downstream
            if rec.type == "claim" and rec.refs and rec.refs[0] == request_id
        ),
        key=lambda rec: rec.id,
    )

    base = None
    # The id of the record `base` was derived from, and whether a turn
    # is running right now. Both exist for the decision overlay below,
    # which must not describe an errand by something older than its
    # newest turn (finding 4 of docs/tasks/0025's code review).
    base_source_id = ""
    turn_is_live = False
    if claims:
        newest_claim = claims[-1]
        if castle.lease_is_held(request_id):
            turn_is_live = True
            base_source_id = newest_claim.id
            base = f"in progress (started {_started_at(castle, newest_claim)})"
        else:
            # The result that closes *this* turn, not merely the newest
            # result on the errand — and the same two-clause rule the
            # dispatch reaper uses, from the same function, so the two
            # surfaces can never disagree about whether a turn was ever
            # accounted for. Clause (b) is what stops this label
            # masking a resident's own hand-written closure behind an
            # "interrupted" the reaper was about to contradict.
            closing = castle.closing_result(newest_claim, results, claims)
            if closing is not None:
                base_source_id = closing.id
                base = _outcome_label(closing.fields.get("outcome", ""), request_id)
            else:
                # The window between "the lease died" and "the next
                # dispatch sweep reaps it into a result". Nothing is
                # running, so "interrupted" is the honest label.
                base_source_id = newest_claim.id
                base = f"interrupted — castle work {request_id} to retry"
    elif results:
        # No claim records anywhere on this errand: a journal from
        # before docs/tasks/0021, where nothing recorded turns at all.
        base_source_id = results[-1].id
        base = _outcome_label(results[-1].fields.get("outcome", ""), request_id)
    elif any(
        rec.type == "decision"
        and rec.fields.get("seat") == castle.DISPATCH_SEAT
        and rec.fields.get("watermark", "").strip()
        # Named in the watermark's own refs, not merely reachable from
        # this errand through one. The watermark excludes BY NAME
        # (docs/tasks/0021 §2.2), so a watermark that named a follow-up
        # request but not this one must not make this one claim it
        # predates dispatch — the same transitive contamination the
        # claim and result selections above are keyed against.
        and request_id in rec.refs
        for rec in downstream
    ):
        # The watermark names the requests it excluded in its own refs
        # (docs/tasks/0021 §2.2), which is exactly why it turns up in
        # this errand's downstream fold — so the reason nothing is
        # happening is already here, and "awaiting a worker" would be
        # 0015's failure again: a label promising a start that is never
        # coming, on an errand automatic dispatch has permanently
        # declined to touch.
        base = f"not started automatically (predates dispatch) — castle work {request_id} to run it"
    elif request.fields.get(castle.FILED_DURING_TURN_FIELD, "").strip():
        # A request a worker tenant filed during its own turn
        # (docs/tasks/0021 §2.4(e)). "Awaiting a worker" would be the
        # 0015 failure exactly: a label promising a start that is never
        # coming, because dispatch deliberately never starts these. Say
        # what is true and what to do about it.
        base = f"filed during a worker turn — castle work {request_id} to run it"

    # A decided proposal relabels the errand
    # (docs/tasks/0025-approval.md §H). Without this the base label
    # underneath is still the newest turn's outcome — "done" — which is
    # precisely the word that must never describe a change the resident
    # REJECTED. The overlay below already clears itself once the
    # proposal is decided (an answer names it, so it is not waiting),
    # and that is the whole problem: clearing the overlay leaves "done"
    # standing alone as the account of an errand whose proposal the
    # resident declined.
    #
    # **Keyed to this request, like `results` and `claims` above and
    # for the identical reason.** `_collect_downstream` is transitive
    # over `refs`, so a follow-up filed with `castle ask --refs R1`
    # drags its own proposal into R1's fold — and without the key, a
    # verdict the resident gave on the FOLLOW-UP's change relabelled
    # its parent: `[R1] requested — you declined this`, about an errand
    # the resident declined nothing on. `refs[0]` is the test because
    # that is the lineage edge a proposal question carries its errand
    # on, the same edge `claims` is keyed by (finding 3 of this task's
    # code review).
    proposals = sorted(
        (
            rec
            for rec in downstream
            if rec.type == "question"
            and rec.fields.get(castle.PROPOSAL_SHA256_FIELD)
            and rec.refs
            and rec.refs[0] == request_id
        ),
        key=lambda rec: rec.id,
    )
    # ACCEPTED LIMIT, and the reason it is not fixed here.
    # `proposals[-1]` below is "newest" only across seconds: ids carry a
    # one-second stamp and a random suffix, so two proposals filed on one
    # errand inside the same second sort arbitrarily, and this can label
    # the errand with the verdict belonging to the other one. Found by
    # the cross-model review, round 3.
    #
    # Not patched, because the bug is the id format rather than this
    # line — see docs/backlog/record-ids-are-only-second-resolution.md,
    # which records that this same hazard has now produced a defect in
    # four separate tasks, including twelve lines below, where the
    # decision-versus-result comparison deliberately compares only the
    # timestamp half to dodge it. Patching one more call site would make
    # the fifth local workaround and still leave the next fold to
    # rediscover it.
    #
    # What bounds the damage: the `waiting` branch at the end of this
    # function is computed independently of this selection, so an errand
    # with an undecided proposal still says "waiting on you — press
    # Mod4+Shift+a to review" whichever proposal got picked. The
    # resident is never left without the remedy; the verdict half of the
    # label can name the wrong one of two changes decided in the same
    # second. Two turns finishing on one errand within one second is a
    # scripted caller or a fast retry, not an ordinary use.
    if proposals and not turn_is_live:
        # **Never over a live turn**, and never over anything newer
        # than the decision itself. This block's own stated rule is
        # that an errand's state is the state of its newest turn, and
        # an unconditional override broke it in two ways: a decision
        # would have described an errand whose next turn is running
        # right now, and — approve, then `castle work` again, then have
        # that turn fail — it would have gone on reading "approved —
        # nothing applied yet" while the failure and its retry command
        # vanished from the surface that exists to show them.
        #
        # Compared on the TIMESTAMP half of the id, not the whole of
        # it, and ties go to the decision. Comparing whole ids read
        # correctly and behaved wrongly: a full id is
        # `<stamp>-<type>-<suffix>`, so within one second the type name
        # decides, `answer` sorts before `result`, and a decision made
        # in the same second as the result it is about looked older
        # than it. That is not a rare case — it is what a scripted
        # caller does every time, and what a resident who approves
        # straight off the notification does too. A tie going to the
        # decision is right on its own terms as well: a decision can
        # only exist after the change it decides, so when the two share
        # a second, the decision is the later event.
        newest_proposal = proposals[-1]
        for rec in sorted(records.values(), key=lambda r: r.id):
            if rec.type != "answer" or newest_proposal.id not in rec.refs:
                continue
            verdict = rec.fields.get("decision", "").strip()
            if not verdict or _stamp_of(rec.id) < _stamp_of(base_source_id):
                continue
            # "approved — nothing applied yet" and not "approved"
            # alone. The same discipline the review surface states to
            # the resident at decision time, restated where a status
            # line would otherwise let "approved" read as "handled".
            # When docs/tasks/0026 can apply an approved change, this
            # is the sentence that task must come back and change —
            # which is the point of writing it this way.
            #
            # An unrecognised value renders verbatim rather than
            # collapsing into any of the three, exactly as
            # `_outcome_label` treats an outcome from outside its own
            # vocabulary: a surface quietly reporting a state because
            # it did not understand what it was told is the failure
            # both closed vocabularies exist to prevent.
            #
            # **"nothing applied yet" is now true only for a change
            # offered before docs/tasks/0026-apply-validate.md.** One
            # carrying `authorizes-apply` is waiting for an applier that
            # exists, so it says so; one without it will never be
            # applied by anything, so the older sentence stays exactly
            # right for it and is kept.
            if verdict == "approve":
                base = (
                    "approved — waiting to be applied"
                    if _authorizes_apply(castle, newest_proposal)
                    else "approved — nothing applied yet"
                )
            else:
                base = {
                    "reject": "you declined this",
                    "defer": "you set this aside",
                }.get(verdict, f"{verdict} — nothing applied yet")

            # **The fourth stage: what then happened to the change.**
            # An apply result closes no claim — it deliberately does not
            # name the request at all (docs/tasks/0026 §F), so
            # `closing_result` and the `results` selection above both
            # correctly ignore it — which means without this, an errand
            # whose apply FAILED would read "approved — waiting to be
            # applied" forever. That is precisely the journal claiming
            # success where validation failed.
            #
            # Inside the enclosing block, so it inherits both of that
            # block's guards for free: never over a live turn, never
            # over anything newer than the decision. It needs no new
            # keying rule either — `_collect_downstream` reaches an
            # apply result through the same `refs[0]` chain that reaches
            # the answer.
            #
            # **Selected by seat and not by the field**, which is a
            # correction: keying on `apply-outcome` alone missed every
            # attempt that failed *before* reaching a conclusion about
            # the change — an unusable checkout, a target role with no
            # checkout, a `git apply` git never finished — all of which
            # write `outcome: failed` with no `apply-outcome` at all.
            # Those records still name the answer, so they still bar it
            # from ever being applied automatically again; the errand
            # then read "approved — waiting to be applied" forever, for
            # something that would never run, with the remedy named
            # nowhere. Verified, not theorised (code review on
            # docs/tasks/0026-apply-validate.md, finding 2).
            #
            # ACCEPTED LIMIT, the same one `proposals[-1]` above carries
            # and inherited rather than worked around: `applies[-1]` is
            # "newest" only across seconds, so a hand retry landing in
            # the same second as the attempt it retries can be described
            # by the wrong one of the two. The bug is the id format —
            # docs/backlog/record-ids-are-only-second-resolution.md,
            # which records that patching one more call site would make
            # the fifth local workaround and still leave the next fold
            # to rediscover it. What bounds the damage here: both
            # records are apply attempts on the same approval, so the
            # label is wrong about which attempt rather than about what
            # kind of thing happened, and the next second resolves it.
            applies = sorted(
                (
                    other
                    for other in downstream
                    if other.type == "result"
                    and other.refs
                    and other.refs[0] == rec.id
                    and other.fields.get("seat") == castle.APPLIER_SEAT
                ),
                key=lambda other: other.id,
            )
            if applies:
                newest_apply = applies[-1]
                outcome = newest_apply.fields.get(castle.APPLY_OUTCOME_FIELD, "").strip()
                if outcome:
                    base = _apply_outcome_label(outcome)
                else:
                    # An attempt that reached no conclusion about the
                    # change. The automatic bar is deliberate and stays
                    # — one attempt per approval, whatever happened — so
                    # what this owes the resident is the hand path,
                    # named, per docs/tasks/0015's rule that a label
                    # must not cause the inaction it describes. It is
                    # also what makes the fail-closed record-door denial
                    # visible: a hand-planted `seat: applier` result is
                    # still refused, but now it says so instead of
                    # reading as an eternal wait.
                    base = f"could not be applied — castle apply {rec.id} to try again"

    # Naming the remedy, the same way every outcome label above does:
    # docs/tasks/0015's rule is that a label must not cause the inaction
    # it describes, and "waiting on you" was the one label on this
    # surface that told the resident something was wrong without telling
    # them what to do about it. CHORD COUPLING: Mod4+Shift+Return is
    # prose here and a real binding in modules/home/default.nix's
    # keybindings attrset — change one and grep for the other
    # (agent/castle's notification carries the identical coupling, and
    # the compose chord's own mentions went stale exactly this way once
    # already, docs/tasks/0019). Since docs/tasks/0034 there is only
    # the one chord: it opens compose, and the waiting item is one Tab
    # away, which the inbox's own count line points at.
    if waiting:
        # "answer" names the wrong act on a proposal: `_show_picker`
        # grew an `action` parameter for exactly this reason
        # (docs/tasks/0025-approval.md §G, called with `action="review"`
        # where a proposal branches out of answer mode's picker), and
        # this overlay used to say "answer" regardless — the same
        # defect the comment above this method already guards against,
        # for a different reason. Pick the verb from what is actually
        # waiting: all proposals says "review" (the same word the
        # picker itself uses, "Press a number to review"); no proposals
        # keeps the original, unchanged "answer"; a mix of both kinds
        # means neither verb is honest for everything waiting, so this
        # falls back to the kind-agnostic phrasing already used where
        # a resident reaches this picker not knowing in advance which
        # kind of waiting it is ("to see what is waiting on them",
        # above `run_answer`'s branch into review mode).
        proposal_flags = [_is_proposal(castle, rec) for rec in waiting_questions]
        if all(proposal_flags):
            verb = "review"
        elif not any(proposal_flags):
            verb = "answer"
        else:
            verb = "see what's waiting"
        hint = f"waiting on you — press Mod4+Shift+Return to {verb}"
        return f"{base}, {hint}" if base else hint
    return base or "awaiting a worker"


def run_status(castle, limit: int, out=sys.stdout) -> int:
    records = castle.load_all(castle.journal_dir())
    requests = sorted(
        (rec for rec in records.values() if rec.type == "request"),
        key=lambda rec: rec.id,
        reverse=True,
    )[:limit]

    if not requests:
        print("No errands yet. Press Mod4+Shift+Return and describe one.", file=out)
        # Both of this mode's exits pause, for the reason compose mode
        # already does (0009 review pass finding 5): the instant
        # anything binds status to a chord, a window with no pause opens
        # and closes in zero human-perceptible frames. Nothing binds one
        # today, but this task's own chord makes that shape one
        # keystroke away, and _pause_for_dismissal is isatty-gated
        # internally, so no piped caller changes behaviour.
        _pause_for_dismissal(out)
        return 0

    for req in requests:
        downstream = castle._collect_downstream(records, req.id)
        state = _errand_state(castle, req, downstream, records)
        first_line = req.body.strip().splitlines()[0] if req.body.strip() else "(no description)"
        print(f"[{req.id}] {req.fields.get('provenance', '?')} — {state}", file=out)
        print(f"    asked: {first_line}", file=out)
        decisions = sorted(
            (rec for rec in downstream if rec.type == "decision"),
            key=lambda rec: rec.id,
        )
        for decision in decisions:
            channel = decision.fields.get("channel", "")
            evidence = decision.fields.get("evidence", "")
            if channel:
                print(f"    decided -> {channel}: {evidence}", file=out)
            else:
                # A decision that routed nothing — today, the dispatch
                # watermark, which names the requests it excluded from
                # automatic starting in its own `refs` and therefore
                # lands in this errand's fold (docs/tasks/0021 §2.2).
                # "decided -> ?" would read as a routing that failed;
                # this reads as what it is, which for an excluded
                # request is exactly the explanation the resident wants
                # for why nothing is happening on its own.
                print(f"    noted: {evidence}", file=out)
        print(file=out)
    _pause_for_dismissal(out)
    return 0


def build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="castle-modal", description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument(
        "--mode",
        choices=("inbox", "compose", "status", "answer", "review"),
        default="compose",
        help=(
            "compose a new request, fold recent errands, answer a question the "
            "system is waiting on, or review a proposed configuration change and "
            "approve, reject or defer it (default: compose). Review mode is "
            "normally reached from answer mode's own picker rather than named "
            "here — a proposed change picked there branches into it "
            "(docs/tasks/0025-approval.md). Inbox is the layout the one chord "
            "opens (docs/tasks/0034): compose and a list of everything waiting, "
            "Tab between them"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--focus",
        default=None,
        help=(
            "inbox mode: open the inbox expanded on this record id. The "
            "notification's own deep link — the one opener that knows why the "
            "window opened — and scripts; never typed by the resident "
            "(docs/tasks/0034)."
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--limit",
        type=int,
        default=10,
        help="status mode: how many recent errands to show (default: 10)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--kind",
        choices=("request", "correction"),
        default="request",
        help=(
            "compose mode, non-interactive callers only: file the composed text as "
            "something to fix (request, the default — every existing piped caller "
            "is unaffected) or as unprompted feedback about how the system is doing "
            "(correction). An interactive session ignores this flag and asks in "
            "plain language instead; this name is for scripts and CI, never shown "
            "to the resident (docs/tasks/0010-correction-record.md scope 6)."
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--question",
        default=None,
        help=(
            "answer mode, non-interactive callers only: the id of the question to "
            "answer, read from stdin in the same '.'-terminated grammar. For scripts "
            "and CI, never shown to the resident — an interactive session ignores "
            "this flag entirely and always shows the picker, because preselecting a "
            "question for a human who is looking at the screen is exactly the "
            "answering-the-wrong-one hazard the picker exists to prevent "
            "(docs/tasks/0022-answer-in-ui.md scope 2). Review mode reads the same "
            "flag under the same rule, and the hazard there is larger: the wrong "
            "guess would be an authorization."
        ),
    )
    return parser


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)
    castle = _load_castle_module()
    if args.mode == "inbox":
        return run_inbox(castle, focus=args.focus)
    if args.mode == "compose":
        return run_compose(castle, kind=args.kind)
    if args.mode == "answer":
        return run_answer(castle, question_id=args.question)
    if args.mode == "review":
        return run_review(castle, question_id=args.question)
    return run_status(castle, args.limit)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
